bibliography: bibliography.bib
abstract: 'In chapter 4 of the first part of the *Astronomia nova* Kepler gives, in eight lines and one figure,
  the argument

  by which Copernicus charged Ptolemy''s equant with making the heavens move unequally: about the centre of its

  circle the planet passes a lesser arc and a greater arc in equal times. We reconstruct this argument. First the

  model is made explicit: every point of Kepler''s figure with its construction and its astronomical meaning, the

  one angle that varies, and what is assumed of the lengths. Then the argument is written as steps, each with the

  premises it uses and the proposition of Euclid that licenses it, none of which Kepler names. The reconstruction

  is checked by a program: every step is tested over the whole parameter space of the model and all its states,

  matched against the rule it cites, and tested for the hypotheses it needs. Three things come out that the

  passage does not say. Kepler argues for the position in which the angle at the equant is right; the central

  inequality holds in every position of the half revolution. The hypothesis that the equant lies as far above the

  centre as the eye lies below it, which Kepler states, is not needed for the conclusion. And the argument rests

  on a triangle that vanishes when the planet is at the apogee or the perigee. A coda treats chapter 2, where

  Kepler states the equivalence of the eccentric and the concentric with epicycle and leaves the proof to the

  figure: "Schema loquitur Geometrae". The paper is meant as a model for doing the same with other passages.

  '
keywords:
