## The passage {#sec-passage}

Chapter 4 of Part I of the *Astronomia nova* [@keplerKGW3] introduces the model that Ptolemy uses for the first
inequality of the planets. Kepler sets it out in three sentences:

> "Centro B scribatur eccentricus DE, cujus eccentricitas sit BA, ut A sit locus oculi. Acta linea per BA
> ostendet in D apogaeum in F perigaeum. In hac linea supra B spatium aliud BC extendatur, aequale ipsi BA. Erit
> C punctum aequantis, punctum nempe, apud quod Planeta aequalibus temporibus conficit aequales angulos, quamvis
> circulum non circa C sed circa B ordinet."

In Donahue's translation [@donahue1992]: "About centre B let an eccentric DE be described, with eccentricity
BA, A being the place of the observer. The line drawn through AB will indicate the apogee at D and the perigee
at F. Upon this line, above B, let another segment BC be extended, equal to BA. C will be the point of the
equant, that is, the point about which the planet completes equal angles in equal times, even though the circle
is set up around B rather than C."

Then comes the argument. Copernicus, Kepler says, censures this hypothesis because it "offends against
physical principles by instituting irregular celestial motions":

> "Eligatur enim E punctum in circulo, quem Planeta corpore peragrat, connectaturque cum CBA : et sit jam DCE
> rectus, ut et ECF. Cum ergo sint anguli hi aequales, constituti nempe aequalibus temporibus, et DCE exterior
> aequet CBE, CEB interiores : ergo parte CEB ablata, residuus CBE vel DBE minor erit quam DCE. itaque FBE major
> quam DCE vel FCE. Sed DE arcus metitur DBE angulum, et EF arcus angulum EBF. minor ergo DE quam EF. et transit
> Planeta per eos aequalibus temporibus."

"Therefore", Kepler concludes, "the entire solid orb is now fast, now slow. This Copernicus rejects as
absurd." The passage stands on printed page 73 of the third volume of the *Gesammelte Werke*; in the Famulus
collection `kepler-prim` it is paragraphs 3 and 4 of page 71 of the document `kgw-03`, and its figure has the
address `fr:kepler-prim/kgw-03/0071/fig01`. Every Latin sentence quoted in this paper was checked against the
transcription of that page by the reading tool described in @sec-check (@tbl-reading).

The argument is short, it is about a model, and it is entirely geometrical: no observation and no number
enters. That makes it a good first case. What does it take to say precisely what Kepler argues, from what, and
by what right?

## The model made explicit {#sec-model}

A reconstruction begins with the model, not with the argument. Kepler's three sentences define a geometry.
Written out, it has six points, one circle, one angle that varies and a few lengths (@tbl-model). We write it in
the language astra/1 [@grasshoff2026astra], in which a model is the pure geometry: it names its lengths and
angles as symbols and gives them no values.

::: {#tbl-model tbl-colwidths="[8,18,34,40]"}

| | what it is | defined by | astronomical meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| R | parameter, length | in (10, 1000) | radius of the eccentric circle about B |
| e | parameter, length | in (0, R / 3) | eccentricity BA: the distance of the eye from the centre |
| q | parameter, length | in (0, R / 3) | the distance BC of the equant point from the centre |
| λ~D~ | parameter, angle | any value | direction of the apogee |
| T | parameter, time | in (100, 20000) | period of the planet about the equant point |
| θ~0~ | parameter, angle | any value | the angle at the equant point at t = 0 |
| θ | kinematic variable | uniform in time, period T | the angle DCE at the equant point, counted from the apogee |
| A | point | `observer()` | the eye |
| B | point | `A + polar(e, lamD)` | centre of the eccentric |
| C | point | `B + polar(q, lamD)` | the equant point |
| orb | circle | `circle(B, R)` | the eccentric, the circle the planet traverses |
| D | point | `B + polar(R, lamD)` | apogee |
| F | point | `B - polar(R, lamD)` | perigee |
| E | point | `seen_from(C, orb, lamD + theta)` | the planet: where the line from C at the angle θ meets the circle |
| λ | observable | `direction(A, E)` | longitude of the planet as seen from A |
| atC | observable | `angle(D, C, E)` | the angle DCE at the equant point |
| atB | observable | `angle(D, B, E)` | the angle DBE at the centre: the arc DE the planet has traversed |

The equant model of Kepler's chapter 4, explicit. Every object has a construction and an astronomical meaning.
The model is `fr:astra/kepler-equant`.
:::

Three decisions were taken in writing the model, and each is a decision about what Kepler says.

*Whose model it is.* Kepler presents the hypothesis as Ptolemy's ("PTOLEMAEVS … operosiori utitur hypothesi").
The model is therefore attributed to Ptolemy; Kepler's paragraph is the witness, the text whose use of the
model is analysed; the description is the author's. These are three different roles.

*What varies.* The planet E is placed where the line from C at the angle θ meets the circle about B. The angle
θ at the equant point is the one kinematic variable. That the planet "completes equal angles in equal times"
about C is the law of this variable: θ grows uniformly with time. Nothing else in the model moves by itself.

*What is a hypothesis.* Kepler makes BC equal to BA. In the model the distance BC is a parameter of its own, q,
beside the eccentricity e. The equality q = e is then a hypothesis of the argument, not a part of the geometry.
This costs nothing and allows a question to be asked later: does the argument need it?

@fig-kepler shows the model drawn from its text in the position Kepler chooses.

![The equant model in the position of Kepler's argument: the angle DCE at the equant point C is right. A is the
eye, B the centre of the circle, D the apogee, F the perigee, E the planet. In gold the lines of the first step:
the triangle CBE with its exterior angle at C. The lengths are drawing values. Kepler's printed figure has the
same points and one more, J, which belongs to a later
argument.](figures/kepler-fig.png){#fig-kepler width=56%}

The same figure in motion, drawn from the model: the angle θ at the equant point C runs through a revolution and
carries the planet E with it. The lengths are drawing values.

{{< famulus fr:astra/kepler-fig-ch4 animation >}}

## The argument reconstructed {#sec-argument}

Kepler's paragraph contains six assertions and two words of inference, "ergo" and "itaque". A reconstruction
makes every inference a step with three parts: the proposition, the premises it is drawn from, and the rule
that licenses the drawing. @fig-argument is the result. It is printed from the same document that drives the
interactive form of the argument.

::: {#fig-argument}

{{< famulus fr:astra/kepler-equant-unequal-motion argument >}}

Kepler's argument about the equant, reconstructed. Indented blocks are sub-arguments: the claim stands first,
the steps that establish it below. At the right the rule of each step; ✓ an instance of the rule named, ◦ read
off the construction, ≈ by calculation. "from" names the premises. H1 is Kepler's hypothesis BC = BA.
:::

The argument has three parts and a supposition.

**The angle at the centre lags behind the angle at the equant (1).** DCE is the exterior angle of the triangle
CBE. Euclid's proposition I.32 says that "the exterior angle is equal to the two interior and opposite angles"
[@heath1908, vol. I, p. 316]: DCE = CEB + CBE. Kepler: "DCE exterior aequet CBE, CEB interiores". Take the part
CEB away and the remainder CBE is less than DCE — "the whole is greater than the part", Common Notion 5. CBE
and DBE are one angle, since C and D lie from B on one ray; Kepler writes "CBE vel DBE". So DBE is less than
DCE.

**The supplement leads (4).** DBE and EBF together make two right angles, and so do DCE and ECF (I.13: "if a
straight line set up on a straight line make angles, it will make either two right angles or angles equal to two
right angles"). Since DBE is less than DCE, EBF is greater than ECF. Kepler: "itaque FBE major quam DCE vel
FCE". His "itaque" carries two applications of I.13 and a subtraction.

**The supposition (5).** "Et sit jam DCE rectus, ut et ECF." Kepler fixes the position: the angle at the equant
is right, so that the planet has used a quarter of its period from D to E and will use another quarter from E
to F. From here on the argument speaks of this position only.

**Unequal arcs in equal times (6).** In this position DCE equals ECF. So DBE is less than DCE, which equals
ECF, which is less than EBF. The arc DE measures the angle DBE at the centre and the arc EF the angle EBF: the
arc DE is less than the arc EF, "et transit Planeta per eos aequalibus temporibus".

Kepler names none of the three propositions. A reader of 1609 did not need them named. A reconstruction does,
because the name of the rule is what makes a step checkable.

## What the checks say {#sec-check}

The reconstruction is a text in a formal language (@lst-derivation), and a program asks four questions of it
[@grasshoff2026fundamentals]. Is every premise there? Is every proposition true — in the model, for every
admissible value of its lengths and every position of the planet? Is every step an instance of the rule it
names, with the hypotheses of that rule among the cited premises and with what the rule takes from the figure
actually holding? And does the conclusion rest only on what is cited?

::: {#lst-derivation}

```
derivation kepler-equant-unequal-motion in kepler-equant "About the centre of its
      circle the planet moves unequally: the arc DE is less than the arc EF, and
      both are passed in equal times"
witness fr:kepler-prim/kgw-03/0071/p05 "Astronomia nova ch. 4"
witness fr:kepler-sec/Kepler1992/0147/p02 "Donahue's translation"
author "Gerd Graßhoff"
given h1: equal(q, e) "the equant point lies as far above the centre as the eye
      lies below it: BC = BA"
show lag: less(angle(D, B, E), angle(D, C, E)) "the angle DBE at the centre is
      less than the angle DCE at the equant point"
  derive k1: equal(angle(E, C, D), angle(C, E, B) + angle(E, B, C)) by Elements
        I.32 "DCE is the exterior angle of the triangle CBE, equal to the interior
        angles CBE and CEB"
  derive k2: less(angle(E, B, C), angle(E, C, D)) from k1 by common notion 5 "the
        part CEB taken away, the remainder CBE is less than DCE"
  derive k3: equal(angle(E, B, C), angle(D, B, E)) from def C, def D, def E by
        construction "CBE or DBE: C and D lie from B on one ray"
  derive k4: less(angle(D, B, E), angle(D, C, E)) from k2, k3 by common notion 1
qed from k4
derive k5: equal(angle(D, B, E) + angle(E, B, F), 180°) by Elements I.13 "BE
      stands on the apsidal line at B: DBE and EBF make two right angles"
derive k6: equal(angle(D, C, E) + angle(E, C, F), 180°) by Elements I.13 "CE
      stands on the apsidal line at C: DCE and ECF make two right angles"
show lead: less(angle(E, C, F), angle(E, B, F)) "so the angle FBE is greater than
      the angle FCE"
  derive k7: less(angle(E, C, F), angle(E, B, F)) from lag, k5, k6 by algebra
qed from k7
suppose quadrant: right_angle(D, C, E) "let DCE be a right angle, and so ECF: two
      quarters of the period"
show unequal: less(angle(D, B, E), angle(E, B, F)) "the arc DE, which measures the
      angle DBE, is less than the arc EF, which measures the angle EBF"
  derive k8: equal(angle(D, C, E), angle(E, C, F)) from quadrant, k6 by algebra
        "the angles at C are equal: they are made in equal times"
  derive k9: less(angle(D, B, E), angle(E, B, F)) from lag, lead, k8 by algebra
        "DBE < DCE = ECF < EBF"
qed from k9
conclude less(angle(D, B, E), angle(E, B, F)) from unequal "in equal times the
      planet passes the lesser arc DE and the greater arc EF: the orb is now slow,
      now fast"
```

The reconstruction as an astra/1 derivation (`fr:astra/kepler-equant-unequal-motion`), comments removed. `show
… qed` is a sub-argument, `suppose` restricts what follows to the positions in which its condition holds.
:::

The answer to all four is yes (@lst-check). Of the nine steps, five are instances of I.32, I.13 and the two
Common Notions, one is read off the construction, three are calculations with the cited equalities and
inequalities. The test runs over 48 sampled sets of lengths and 72 positions of the planet.

::: {#lst-check}

```
$ famulus-astra derive kepler-equant-unequal-motion
kepler-equant-unequal-motion: derivation in kepler-equant, level conclusive (5/5)
  ✓ well-formed
  ✓ grounded
  ✓ true
  ✓ justified
  ✓ conclusive
  given     h1: equal(q, e)
  ✓ lag: less(angle(D, B, E), angle(D, C, E)) from k4 — justified (it is k4)
    ✓ k1: equal(angle(E, C, D), angle(C, E, B) + angle(E, B, C)) by Elements I.32
          — justified (configuration conditions fail there: noncollinear(E, B, C))
      degenerate at theta = 0°, theta = 180°
    ✓ k2: less(angle(E, B, C), angle(E, C, D)) from k1 by common notion 5 —
          justified
    ✓ k3: equal(angle(E, B, C), angle(D, B, E)) from def C, def D, def E by
          construction — read off the construction
    ✓ k4: less(angle(D, B, E), angle(D, C, E)) from k2, k3 by common notion 1 —
          justified
  ✓ k5: equal(angle(D, B, E) + angle(E, B, F), 180°) by Elements I.13 — justified
  ✓ k6: equal(angle(D, C, E) + angle(E, C, F), 180°) by Elements I.13 — justified
  ✓ lead: less(angle(E, C, F), angle(E, B, F)) from k7 — justified (it is k7)
    ✓ k7: less(angle(E, C, F), angle(E, B, F)) from lag, k5, k6 by algebra — by
          calculation
  · quadrant: right_angle(D, C, E) — supposed
  ✓ unequal: less(angle(D, B, E), angle(E, B, F)) from k9 — justified (it is k9)
    ✓ k8: equal(angle(D, C, E), angle(E, C, F)) from quadrant, k6 by algebra — by
          calculation
    ✓ k9: less(angle(D, B, E), angle(E, B, F)) from lag, lead, k8 by algebra — by
          calculation
  ✓ conclusion: less(angle(D, B, E), angle(E, B, F)) from unequal — justified (it
        is unequal)
  note: given h1 (equal(q, e)) is not needed for the conclusion
  note: degenerate states, in which a step's rule does not apply, a term is
