```

The report of the checks.
:::

The quotations are checked separately. Each sentence of Kepler that the reconstruction rests on is an item of a
reading file: the quotation is searched in the transcription of the paragraph, and the assertion is tied to the
step that renders it (@tbl-reading).

::: {#tbl-reading tbl-colwidths="[4,12,46,30,8]"}

| # | place | quoted | checked as | verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `0071/p04` | Erit C punctum aequantis, punctum nempe, apud quod Planeta aequalibus temporibus conficit aequa… | proposition `p1` | ✓ |
| 2 | `0071/p04` | spatium aliud BC extendatur, aequale ipsi BA | not formal `—` | — |
| 3 | `0071/p05` | DCE exterior aequet CBE, CEB interiores | step `k1` | ✓ |
| 4 | `0071/p05` | ergo parte CEB ablata, residuus CBE vel DBE minor erit quam DCE | step `lag` | ✓ |
| 5 | `0071/p05` | itaque FBE major quam DCE vel FCE | step `lead` | ✓ |
| 6 | `0071/p05` | minor ergo DE quam EF. et transit Planeta per eos aequalibus temporibus | step `unequal` | ✓ |
| 7 | `0071/p05` | Quod COPERNICVS ut absurdum rejicit | not formal `—` | — |
| 8 | `0064/p04` | eccentricus paria facit cum epicyclo in concentrico | step `conclusion` | ✓ |
| 9 | `0064/p04` | hic semidiameter epicycli aequet illic eccentricitatem, semidiametri vero illic eccentri et hic… | not formal `—` | — |
| 10 | `0064/p05` | Dico, distantias AC | step `same` | ✓ |
| 11 | `0065/p02` | Nec verbis opus est. Schema loquitur Geometrae. | not formal `—` | — |

The sentences of Kepler used in this paper, with the paragraph where each stands and the step or proposition
it is checked as. ✓ confirmed; — a statement that is not a step of the geometry. Items 8 to 11 belong to
chapter 2 (@sec-coda).
:::

## What the reconstruction shows {#sec-shows}

Three things come out of the checks that Kepler's paragraph does not say.

**The inequality holds in every position, not only at the right angle.** Kepler introduces the right angle
before he states the exterior angle. In the reconstruction the supposition stands where it is first needed,
after the two inequalities, and the checks confirm that it can stand there: the angle at the centre is less
than the angle at the equant in every position of the planet between apogee and perigee, and its supplement is
greater (@fig-states). The right angle is needed for one thing only: to make the two angles at C equal, so that
two arcs can be compared that are passed in equal times. What Kepler shows for two quarters of the period is a
property of the model throughout: measured from the apogee, the planet has always turned less about the centre
of its circle than about the equant point.

![The first inequality in three positions: the angle DCE at the equant is 40°, 90° and 140°. In each the angle
DBE at the centre is the smaller one. Kepler argues the middle position.](figures/kepler-three-states.png){#fig-states width=100%}

**The bisection is not needed.** Kepler states that BC is equal to BA, and this is what makes the model
Ptolemy's. For every hypothesis the program repeats the test with that hypothesis lifted, here with the equant
at any distance above the centre. The conclusion still holds, and so does every step. The report notes: "given
h1 is not needed for the conclusion". The argument against the equant does not turn on where the equant point
lies, only on its not being the centre. That is worth knowing when one reads on: the next pages of the chapter
compare Ptolemy's model with Copernicus's replacement, and there the bisection matters.

**At the apsides the triangle vanishes.** I.32 speaks of a triangle. When the planet is at D or at F, the
points C, B and E lie on one line and there is none. The program evaluates, for every step that is an instance
of a rule, what the rule takes from the figure — here that E, B, C form a triangle and that C lies between B
and D — in every sampled position, and reports the positions in which it fails: θ = 0° and θ = 180°. In these
two positions the strict inequality becomes an equality, as it must. Kepler's argument does not touch them,
since it supposes the right angle; the general statement of the previous paragraph has to exclude them.

None of this corrects Kepler. It states what his argument establishes, how far beyond his chosen position it
reaches, and which of the things he says he uses.

## Coda: "the figure speaks to the geometer" {#sec-coda}

Two chapters earlier Kepler treats the simplest equivalence of all: an eccentric circle produces the same as an
epicycle on a concentric, "ubi eccentricus paria facit cum epicyclo in concentrico", provided that the line of
apsides in the one and the line from the centre of the epicycle to the planet in the other stay parallel, that
"hic semidiameter epicycli aequet illic eccentricitatem", and that the radii of the eccentric and of the
concentric are equal. He draws two figures (@fig-ch2), states what is to be shown — "Dico, distantias AC, αγ,
aequales esse" and the angles at the eye likewise — and then stops:

> "Hoc inquam PTOLEMAEVS demonstravit lib. III. Nec verbis opus est. Schema loquitur Geometrae."

Ptolemy has demonstrated this; no words are needed; the figure speaks to the geometer.

![Kepler's two figures for chapter 2, drawn from the two models with his letters: left the concentric with
epicycle (the eye A, the centre B of the epicycle, the planet C), right the eccentric (the eye α, the centre β,
the planet γ). The lengths are drawing values.](figures/kepler-ch2.png){#fig-ch2 width=92%}

What the figure says can be written down. The two models are those of Ptolemy's solar theory, and their
equivalence has been reconstructed elsewhere from Swerdlow's exposition of it [@grasshoff2026twocases]. Laid on
one another with one eye, the two figures form a parallelogram: the radius of the eccentric to the planet and
the radius of the concentric to the centre of the epicycle are equal and parallel, so the lines that join their
ends are equal and parallel (Elements I.33), and the planet of the one model is the planet of the other.
@fig-overview gives the argument at its coarsest depth.

::: {#fig-overview}

{{< famulus fr:astra/solar-equivalence-equal-radii argument >}}

The equivalence of the eccentric and the concentric with epicycle, at the depth *overview*: the claim, that it
is argued by cases, and the conclusion. The full argument has twenty-six steps in three cases.
:::

Two things in it are of interest here. Kepler's three conditions are exactly what the reconstruction needs: the
parallel lines are part of the construction of the epicyclic model, and the two equalities of lengths are its
two hypotheses; the test with each lifted finds that the conclusion needs both. And the figure does not speak
in every position. When the planet is at the apogee or at the perigee, the parallelogram collapses into the
line of apsides, and the argument needs two further cases, in which lengths along one line are added and
subtracted. A figure drawn in a general position cannot say that.

## How to reconstruct an argument {#sec-how}

The procedure used here is the same for any geometrical passage about a model.

1. **Fix the passage.** Take the text from an edition with addressable paragraphs; quote it; have the quotations
   checked against the transcription.
2. **Write the model.** Every point, line and circle of the figure gets a construction from what precedes it,
   and a meaning. Lengths and angles are symbols. Decide what varies. Say whose model it is.
3. **Separate hypotheses from geometry.** What the author assumes of the lengths, and could have assumed
   otherwise, is a hypothesis of the argument.
4. **Write the steps.** One proposition per step, in the author's order, in the author's words as its gloss.
   For every "therefore" name the premises and find the proposition that licenses it. Where the author fixes a
   position, write a supposition, and place it where it is first needed.
5. **Run the checks.** Grounded, true, justified, conclusive.
6. **Read the report as a finding about the text.** Which steps are instances of propositions, which are read
   off the figure, which hypotheses are needed, which positions are not covered.

The result is a text that can be read at three depths: the claim and its main parts; every step with its rule;
and every step with the binding of its rule, what the rule takes from the figure, and where it fails.

## Limits {#sec-limits}

The checks test truth on samples of the parameter space and of the positions; they do not prove. A step counts
as an instance of a proposition when it matches a schema of that proposition; the schema states one case, and
the wording of the proposition was compared with Heath's translation [@heath1908], not with the Greek. Three of
the nine steps are calculations that the program confirms numerically without replaying them. Arcs are compared
through the angles at the centre that measure them, as Kepler does; the length of an arc is not computed.

The reconstruction covers eight lines of chapter 4. The same chapter goes on to Copernicus's replacement of the
equant by a second epicycle, to the remark that the planet's path is then not a circle, and to a numerical
comparison of the two models for Mars; these are arguments of the same kind and are not treated here. The
physical reading that Kepler attaches to the equant in the following paragraph is not a geometrical argument
and lies outside this method.

The Latin is the transcription of the collection, produced by optical character recognition and checked here
only for the quoted sentences. The volume's record in the collection names its editors and publisher and no
year; none is given in the bibliography.

## Conclusion

Eight lines of Kepler became a model with six points, one hypothesis, one supposition and nine steps under
three propositions of Euclid and two Common Notions. Checked, the argument is sound as far as it goes, and it
goes further than Kepler takes it: the inequality between the angle at the centre and the angle at the equant
holds in every position, and it does not depend on the bisection of the eccentricity. Where Kepler leaves an
argument to the figure, as in chapter 2, the reconstruction says what the figure says and where it falls silent.

## Availability {.unnumbered}

The model `fr:astra/kepler-equant`, the derivation `fr:astra/kepler-equant-unequal-motion`, the figure texts and
the reading file `readings/kepler-an-ch4.yaml` are in the Famulus workspace `famulus-astronomica`
(`examples/kepler/`). The interactive form of the argument is the element `<astra-argument>`; the page version
of this issue shows it as @fig-argument. The language and the checks are described in
[@grasshoff2026astra] and [@grasshoff2026fundamentals]. Kepler is cited by the pages of the Famulus collection
`kepler-prim`, document `kgw-03` (page n of the collection is printed page n + 2); Donahue's translation by the
collection `kepler-sec`, document `Kepler1992`.
